      <p>Spotify is a fourth option worth a sentence. Its Premium tier includes a set number of audiobook listening hours each month, a standalone Audio Access plan exists for audiobooks alone, and an Audiobooks+ add-on extends the hours further. The trade-off is the same as everywhere else in this category: hours instead of ownership.</p>

      <h2>What the Rebrand Means for EPUB Ownership</h2>

      <p>Here is the uncomfortable core of every service in the table above. A subscription is a licence, not a purchase. You are borrowing. When the subscription ends, so does your access, and the file on your device — if you have one at all — is wrapped in DRM and bound to your account. If Amazon, Rakuten, or Audible changes its catalog rules, your library changes with it.</p>

      <p>The Kindle+ rebrand sharpens that point in a specific and slightly uncomfortable way. Amazon is now selling the <strong>combination</strong> of a book and its audio rendering as one subscription object. The unit of value is no longer the file. It is the experience, delivered in whatever format the current pipeline produces, regenerated on demand, tied to a service that can rename itself without notice. Owning a durable, portable file — a plain EPUB that opens in any reader on any device in ten years — stops being the default and becomes a deliberate counter-position.</p>

      <p>If you have been putting that off, there are four practical routes, and they are not mutually exclusive.</p>

      <p><strong>Buy the file, not the licence.</strong> If you want to own an ebook permanently, look for stores that ship DRM-free EPUB files directly. Amazon's own <a href="/blog/amazon-drm-free-epub-download-2026/">DRM-free EPUB download program</a> now covers a meaningful slice of its catalog, and our <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free EPUB library guide</a> covers the wider open ecosystem, including <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/">Project Gutenberg</a>.</p>

      <p><strong>Migrate what you already own.</strong> If you have accumulated Kindle books over the years and want them in a format that survives an account closure, our <a href="/blog/migrate-kindle-library-to-epub/">Kindle library to EPUB migration guide</a> walks through the practical steps, and <a href="/blog/calibre-vs-sigil-2026/">Calibre versus Sigil</a> compares the two best editing tools for the job.</p>

      <p><strong>Convert your own documents.</strong> A large share of ebook-shaped reading is not published books at all — it is manuals, reports, research papers, and course material. If you own the source file, you already own the content, and converting it to a clean, reflowable EPUB with <a href="https://converter-epub.com/">our free browser-based converter</a> takes seconds without uploading anything to a server.</p>

      <p><strong>Turn your EPUB into audio.</strong> The obvious counter to a catalog of synthetic narration is your own text. Our <a href="/blog/epub-to-audiobook-ai-2026/">EPUB to audiobook guide</a> covers the open tooling for narrating a book you own, which is a very different proposition from borrowing one that somebody else generated.</p>

      <p>For authors rather than readers, the rebrand is a strategic signal worth tracking. We have written about <a href="/blog/why-authors-leaving-amazon-2026/">why authors leave Amazon</a> and how <a href="/blog/amazon-kdp-drm-opt-in-2026/">KDP's DRM-free opt-in</a> works, and the KDP Select auto-enrolment of AI audiobooks fits that same pattern: the trade terms that keep a title exclusive inside Amazon are increasingly the terms that keep its audiobook version exclusive inside Amazon too.</p>

      <h2>What to Do Right Now</h2>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Check what you are paying and what you are getting.</strong> Log into your Amazon account and confirm the membership is listed as Kindle+ at $11.99 per month, and confirm you are not also paying for Prime Reading under the impression that the two libraries are the same. They are not.</li>
        <li><strong>Decide whether you want access or ownership.</strong> If you read heavily and rarely finish what you borrow, a subscription is a rational choice. If you want a library that survives the subscription, ownership is the only thing that works. You cannot convert a borrowed title into an owned one later.</li>
        <li><strong>Test the audio side before you commit to the new bundle.</strong> The rebrand's headline feature is audiobook access, and the catalog is heavily populated with Virtual Voice narration. Listen to a few AI-narrated samples on a borrowed title before deciding the expansion is worth anything to you.</li>
        <li><strong>Export anything you want to keep.</strong> If you have Kindle-encumbered books you have already paid for, or personal notes and highlights tied to your account, export them now rather than at cancellation time.</li>
        <li><strong>Authors: audit your KDP Select enrolment.</strong> If your ebook is in KDP Select, an AI-narrated audiobook version may already be sitting in the Kindle+ catalog. Decide deliberately whether you want that, and check how <a href="https://kdp.amazon.com/en_US/help/topic/G200634500">KDP Select</a> exclusivity interacts with the wider audiobook rights you hold.</li>
      </ol>

