      <h2>Bottom Line</h2>

      <p>Kindle Unlimited becoming Kindle+ is a better name for a broader product, and the mechanical details that readers actually care about — the price, the 20 borrows, the absence of due dates, the device support — all survived intact. The strategically important part is the one buried in the announcement: Amazon is bundling a book's audio rendering into the same subscription object, generating much of it with AI, and enrolling it automatically for authors who signed up to KDP Select for very different reasons.</p>

      <p>If you are a reader, the practical question is unchanged even though the branding is new: do you want access or ownership? For most people the answer is both, in different places. Keep the subscription for the discovery and the convenience, and keep your permanent library somewhere that is actually yours — DRM-free EPUB files on your own disk, converted from sources you control. That way a rebrand, a price rise, or an account closure becomes an inconvenience rather than a loss.</p>
